;; ========
;; * Tips *
;; ========
;;
;; ** Why Emacs shortcuts involve Ctrl?
;;
;;   Emacs is a rather old piece of Unix software. The keyboard used by Emacs
;;  developers at MIT AI Lab in the late 1970s differed from the ANSI and  ISO layouts. 
;;  The position of the Ctrl on the keyboard was another.
;;   This is one of the main reasons why I often receive the same while sharing
;;  this delightful practice with developers: “Why is it so painful?” But things
;;  are never (almost never) as bad as they seem.
;;
;; ** How to remap CapsLock to Ctrl and enjoy Emacs?
;;
;;   Before we start learning the Emacs keyboard, let's remap CapsLock to Ctrl.
;;  This key swapping prevents you from accidentaly breaking your finger(s) and
;;  undermining your motivation.
;;
;;   1. If you are Windows user, use the "SharpKeys" free tool to remap your keyboard.
;;   2. If you are GNU/Linux user:
;;    2.2. If you are GNOME 3+ user, then run or install Gnome Tweeks. Find a keyboard
;;       Settings, and remap the CapsLock button with the Left Ctrl.
;;    2.2. If you have ANY other environment, put this command:
;;          $ setxkbmap -option "ctrl:nocaps"
;;         into one of your's startup scripts. For example ".bashrc"...
;;
;; ** How to set keyboard latency?
;;
;;   Another tip for smoother typing: open the terminal and change the
;;  keyboard delay/repeat rate. Put the next command to the terminal:
;;
;;     $ xset r rate 200 130
;;
;;  where 200 — is a delay between the pressed key on the keyboard and the
;;  symbol showing on the screen. 130 — the keyboard repeat rate. Both values should
;;  be adapted to your hardware.
;;
;; ** How to get rid of nausea at the sight of default Emacs?
;;
;;   Put a Fiat Multipla, Nissan Juke, or any car made by USSR on your desktop wallpaper.
;;  After that, when you see Emacs, you should feel better.
;;

;; ===================
;; * Emacs Shortcuts *
;; ===================
;;
;; !!!! Once you have remapped Ctrl, open Emacs, and let’s start with
;; !!!! shortcuts and aliases.
;;
;;  M - Alt
;;  C - Ctrl
;;  S - Shift
;;  <SPC> - Space
;;  <Del> - Enter
;;  s (small) - Super Linux key, never bind it (or “Windows” button on Windows OS),
;;      because it is a system key and using it can lead to some issues.
;;
;;  Movement is probably the hardest part and it may be scary and daunting.
;; Anyway, give your brain at least 15 minutes to deal with the text in the
;; open buffer. The key is to become accustomed to using it regularly. With
;; practice, it can become second nature and even an exciting activity for
;; those who enjoy accidentally hitting their toe on the corner of the bed.
;;
;; I recommend familiarizing yourself with the Emacs Reference Card ->
;;    https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/refcards/pdf/refcard.pdf
;;
;; RefCard contains a list of all default shortcuts. Check it to find
;; shortcuts to enhance your Emacs experience.
;;
;; The amazing Emacs community and powerful knowledge base can help you find
;; many tips on how to deal with simple editing routines, and what is even more
;; important, how to customize Emacs.
;;
;; Hint: You will be amazed at how many out-of-the-box things Emacs already has;
;;       you just need to bind them to hotkeys.
;;
;; ---------------
;; ** Movements **
;; ---------------
;;
;;  C-f (right) M-f (word right)
;;  C-b (left)  M-b (word back)
;;  C-n (down)  C-p (up)
;;  C-a (start line)
;;  C-e (end line)
;;  C-m (new line or Enter)
;;  C-v (scroll down)
;;  M-v (scroll up)
;;  M-S-. (go to the end of the buffer)
;;  M-S-, (go to start of the buffer)
;;
;; -------------------------------------------------
;; ** Deleting / Coping and other editing hints **
;; -------------------------------------------------
;;
;;  C-d (delete character in front)
;;  M-d (delete word in front)
;;  C-k (kill line to end)
;;  C-S-minus (undo/redo)
;;  C-<SPC> (start selection text)
;;  C-g (escape, discard, or unselect something, the main exit function)
;;  C-w (crop), M-w (copy), C-y (paste)
;;
;;   In most editors, when we want to remove a symbol, we typically start at the
;;  end of the text we are going to delete and press the Backspace key or
;;  Ctrl+Backspace. This also applies to Emacs, but as an alternative (which I
;;  strongly recommend), you can delete text in the forward direction using C-k,
;;  C-d or M-d. Keep in mind that in Emacs, deleting text is a crop operation,
;;  which can be annoying: it saves the deleted text in the system clipboard,
;;  clearing previously copied text from the buffer.
;;
;; ---------------
;; ** Searching **
;; ---------------
;;
;;  C-s (search in front)
;;  C-r (search in backward)
;;  C-S-s (search regexp in front)
;;  C-S-r (search regexp in backward)
;;  M-u (uppercase)
;;  M-l (lowercase)
;;  M-c (capitalize)
;;  C-t (swap characters) M-t (swap words)
;;  C-l (get the screen relative to the current cursor position)
;; 
;;  In Emacs, editing is closely linked to the search process. If you need to
;;  edit the word "escape", which is four lines above your current cursor
;;  position, you have two options:
;; 
;;  1. press 'C-p' to move up, and then 'C-b'
;; 
;;  2. the quickest method is to use 'C-r' (searching backward); hold 'Ctrl' and
;;     press 'r' repeatedly until you find the "escape" word you want to edit.
;;     Try this for faster navigation in Emacs.
;;
;; -----------------------
;; ** Window management **
;; -----------------------
;;
;;  A quick note about Emacs terminology:
;;
;;  `Buffer' - in Emacs contains the content that you edit/see/modify. It is
;;  similar to what other systems refer to as a file, document, workspace, or
;;  editor. When you open a file, Emacs buffer makes its clone and opens it for
;;  modification. Every buffer may be wrapped with Major/Minor mode. You can also
;;  open a system buffer (which doesn't display any File or Document) and use it, 
;;  for example, or customizing or debugging.
;;
;;  For instance, ‘customize-theme’ opens a system buffer, or *Messages* buffer
;;  that outputs Emacs commands. System buffer (in most cases) has asteriks ‘*’
;;  around its name, like “*Custom Themes*”. It’s just a name without any
;;  special effects on the user experience.

;;  `Window' - is a buffer container that can be shrunk, closed, or
;;  swapped. Windows can display parts of different buffers or different
;;  parts of a single buffer.
;;
;;  `Frame' -  is a screen object provided by X-Org or by Windows (referred to as a
;;  “window” in the Windows ecosystem). Emacs can split a frame into
;;  multiple windows.
;;
;;  C-x 3 (split window horizontally)
;;  C-x 2 (split window vertically)
;;  C-x 0 (close split window)
;;  C-x o (move to other window)
;;  C-x 5 2 (create new frame)
;;  C-x 5 0 (close selected frame)
;;
;; ---------------
;; ** Font size **
;; ---------------
;;
;;  C-x C-= (increase font size)
;;  C-x C-- (decrease font size)
;;
;; -----------------
;; ** System keys **
;; -----------------
;;
;;  M-x (modebar action)
;;  C-x C-f (find-and-open file or create it)
;;  C-x C-s (save file)
;;  C-x C-c (exit Emacs)
;;  C-x k (kill buffer, like close file/tab in other editors)
;;  C-x b (choose buffer you need)
;;  C-x d (dired file manager)
;;  C-x C-b (advanced buffer management, to Exit press 'q)
;;  C-h b (list of ALL keybindings supported IN THE CURRENTLY SELECTED BUFFER)
;;
;;  Ok, that's all you need to know.
;;  Now, let's configure your Emacs.
;;
;; ----------------------------------------
;; ** Getting help without leaving Emacs **
;; ----------------------------------------
;; 
;;  Emacs offers a wide range of help functions. To access them, press
;;  ‘F1’ or ‘C-h’. Emacs will then wait for your next key/shortcut to open
;;  a help-buffer, which will assist you with your issue. I recommend 
;;  using ‘C-h C-h’ to open the buffer that will redirect you to the page you 
;;  are searching for. Alternatively, you can use more specific help views:
;;  
;;  C-h b (opens a buffer with a list of all currently available keybindings for you)
;;  C-h t (opens a tutorial that will help you navigate Emacs using keybindings
;;  C-h r (large Emacs manual)
;;  C-h f (describes function)
;;  C-h v (describes variable)
;; 
;; ----------------------------
;; ** Compiling inside Emacs **
;; ----------------------------
;; 
;;  Scripts and the large internal mechanics (configuration, etc.) are
;;  implemented with the Emacs Lisp Compiler. Modifying Emacs behavior by
;;  editing its source code could be a challenging task. Fortunately, the
;;  creators of Emacs built a compiler within the program, allowing users to
;;  compile something directly within their files. This means that you can 
;;  enhance Emacs behavior and change its appearance on-the-fly. But how?
;;  
;;  To add a function to the global namespace or perform calculations, open Emacs
;;  Lisp buffer. Create a file with the ‘.el’ extension or use the existing
;;  scratch buffer. Then, write your code and evaluate it using `C-x C-e'.
;;
;; 
;;      (+ 10 (+ 10 10) )
;;                       ^
;;  .--------------------'
;;  '- put your cursor here (after the bracket) and press C-x C-e
;;     to evaluate s-expression, and the result you can see inside 
;;     minibuffer =>  "30 (#o36, #x1e, ?\C-^)" . Try to evaluate the
;;     internal sum expression. 
;; 
;;  C-x C-e (eval s-expression right before your cursor place)
;;  C-M-x (eval whole s-expression to the top-level, not depend where IN
;;         the brackets your cursor is)
;;
;;  If you want to compile the entire file, use `M-x eval-buffer'. 

;; =======================
;; * Emacs Configuration *
;; =======================
;;
;;  First, create a ~/.emacs file in your home dir or open an existing one.
;;
;;  Place the following text at the top of the file. The 'package' is needed to load
;;  packages from remote repositories and compile those already downloaded from
;;  the ~/.emacs.d/elpa folder.

(require 'package)
(load "package")
(package-initialize)

;;  Next, set a list of repositories. Let’s organize all of them in a variable.
;;  We will use it a few lines later to streamline config file management.

(setq repository-list
      '(("melpa" . "https://melpa.org/packages/")
	("gnu" . "https://elpa.gnu.org/packages/")
	("org" . "http://orgmode.org/elpa/")
	;; ("melpa-stable" . "https://stable.melpa.org/packages/")
	))

;;  In the default configuration, Emacs doesn't save selected themes, resetting
;; the user's choice with each startup. Therefore, set the `custom-safe-themes' on
;; true. Make it the first step during program startup.
;; Note! In ELisp, only `nil' is real false value. True value is `t' symbol.

(setq custom-safe-themes t)

;;  After setting this variable, the program will no longer ask you about the
;; previously chosen theme. Variables related to Emacs built-in functionality
;; can be placed either before `custom-set-variables' macros (using 'setq')
;; body or inside it. Example:
;;
;;  (custom-set-variables
;;   '(custom-safe-themes t))

;;  The next .emacs lines should be: custom-set-variables,
;; custom-set-faces. These are macros,where EMACS KEEPS & CONTROLS ALL
;; SETTINGS. It offers a large number of setting-variables, such as previously
;; mentioned ‘custom-safe-themes’ which allows you to tailor Emacs behavior
;; to your preferences.

(custom-set-variables
 ;; custom-set-variables was added by Custom.
 ;; If you edit it by hand, you could mess it up, so be careful.
 ;; Your init file should contain only one such instance.
 ;; If there is more than one, they won't work correctly.
 '(package-archives repository-list))

(custom-set-faces
 ;; custom-set-faces was added by Custom.
 ;; If you edit it by hand, you could mess it up, so be careful.
 ;; Your init file should contain only one such instance.
 ;; If there is more than one, they won't work right.
 )

;; ---------------------------------------------
;; ** Emacs UI, or Let’s set default behavior **
;; ---------------------------------------------
;;
;;   Stay a true keyboard warrior and don't touch your mouse! Let’s get rid of
;;  unnecessary UI components that require mouse interactions.

(scroll-bar-mode -1)
(tool-bar-mode   -1)
(menu-bar-mode   -1)

;;   Unlike most text editors, Emacs doesn't display line numbers
;;  by default Emacs doesn’t bother with informing users about row
;;  numbers. 
;;  To display them, enable the corresponding mode.

(global-display-line-numbers-mode 1)

;;  Threshold settings force Emacs to open another buffer in a horizontally
;;  split window. If you are using standard FullHD monitor (or smaller),
;;  just keep standard Emacs behavior and delete the following the lines.

(setq split-width-threshold 0)
(setq split-height-threshold nil)

;; Emacs makes backups the first time you create a file and also when you
;; save it. Autosaving, backuping, seat belts make our life so boring and
;; predictable. So let's disable them (and use C-x C-s for saving)

(setq make-backup-files nil)

;; Integrate Emacs clipboard with the system XOrg clipboard (only for Linux users)

(delete-selection-mode t)
(transient-mark-mode t)
(setq x-select-enable-clipboard t)

;; The C-z shortcut freezes the frame, allowing you to return to the shell where
;; the program was invoked. But in practice, it can painfully disrupts the
;; programming flow and developers’ mental health.

;; We will unpin and reuse it for further customizations.

(global-unset-key (kbd "C-z"))


;;  Switching between split windows in Emacs can be frustrating 
;; for beginners. We can resolve it by remapping this highly needed
;; functionality to other keys.

(defun other-window-back ()
  (interactive)
  (other-window -1))

;; `C-x o' replacement
(global-set-key (kbd "C-o") 'other-window)
(global-set-key (kbd "C-i") 'other-window-back)


;; ==================
;; * Emacs Packages *
;; ==================
;;
;; What is an Emacs package? In short, it's Emacs Lisp code that integrates or
;; overrides specific Emacs behaviors or provides public functions for further
;; usage. Press the M-x command to find it in the global namespace.
;;
;; -------------------------------
;; ** How to install something? **
;; -------------------------------
;;
;;   Before we start, let’s review an installation case for a specific package,
;;   'dashboard extension which replaces our "Welcome buffer" with a new, more
;;   organized view. An indispensable thing, believe me :)
;;
;;   0. press 'M-x' and...
;;   1. Type `package-refresh-contents' to refresh the list of packages
;;      from the remote repositories we specified earlier (melpa,elpa,melpa-stable)
;;   2. Once the list of available packages is updated, press 'M-x' and type
;;      'package-install' - your action-bar will be in waiting mode until you enter
;;      the name of the package you want to install.
;;      So, type ‘dashboard’, press '<DEL>' (Enter) and wait a few seconds until the
;;      package retrieves all the parts and compiles (loads) them.
;;
;;      Note! To delete a package use `package-delete'
;;
;;   3. ...but what comes next? We must USE this package directly.
;;      Use ‘require’ statement that loads downloaded code in Emacs for its
;;      further use:
;;      (You can use 'loading' term for describing compiling in Emacs)
;;
;;         (require 'dashboard)
;;
;;      This line compiles the package after Emacs reads the ~/.emacs file.
;;
;;      Next, we can add another specific settings, required or optional (depends on
;;      the package we use). For example, if you don't need Agenda view,
;;      the 'dashboard package can pull out customizable "setting variables". (`setq'
;;      - set variable value, `nil'/`t' is False/True equivalent in ELisp)
;;
;;         (setq dashboard-week-agenda nil)
;;
;;      Finally, to activate dashboard, invoke it
;;
;;         (dashboard-setup-startup-hook)
;;
;;   4. All you need is three lines, but only limited by them we will accumulate
;;      dozens of packages with configs and quickly become clutter our ~/.emacs.
;;      Actually, the package `use-package' was created to help us organize
;;      configurations. Let’s gather `dashboard' configs together.
;;
;;         (use-package dashboard
;;            :ensure t
;;            :init (setq dashboard-week-agenda nil)
;;            :config (dashboard-setup-startup-hook))
;;
;;      This approach keeps config in one large expression, but adds some extra
;;      features, for example `:ensure t` automatically installs package if it can
;;      not be found in local storage (~/emacs.d/elpa)
;;      For making this organizational trick we must install a use-package using
;;      `package-refresh-contents' and `package-install' or evaluating the code I
;;      prepared below.
;;
;;  So let then install 'use-package:

(unless (package-installed-p 'use-package)
  (progn
    (package-refresh-contents)
    (package-install 'use-package)))

;;
;; -----------------------------------
;; ** Few words about 'use-package' **
;; -----------------------------------
;;
;;  As mentioned, it helps us organize configurations, reduce Emacs
;;  startup time, and control the loading process.
;;
;;    (use-package <package-name-symb>)
;;
;;  The previous declaration compiles code from local repositories and puts
;;  <package-related> functions in the common Emacs Lisp namespace, equivalent 
;;  to the `require' Emacs function (require <package-name-symb>)
;;
;;  Use-package provides even more control with the following Keywords:
;;  `:ensure' - if the package is not installed, it will be downloaded 
;;     and installed into the local repository.
;;  `:init' - the next code segment will be evaluated before the corresponding
;;     package is compiled
;;  `:config' - the next code will be evaluated after the package is loaded
;;  `:defer' |`:demand' (t|nil) - both rely on 'autoload' Emacs feature. Some
;;    installed packages can be quite complex, consisting of dozens of heavy files. For
;;     example, Major mode can contain hundreds of functions that are not required
;;     to be compiled and loaded immediately. You don’t need a full list of
;;     java-mode interactive functions if you plan to work with Clojure
;;     files. Package authors mostly use the autoload feature for the mode-toggle
;;     function to make it visible (when loading Emacs package, only functions
;;     marked as 'autoload' are added to the common namespace and can be invoked
;;     using M-x; the other functions are not loaded until you manually invoke
;;     java-mode, just as an example). :defer and :demand keys gіve us control over
;;     this process:
;;       `:defer' - defers the evaluation of the entire library until you use it
;;             directly. In most cases, you don’t need to use it manually.
;;       `:demand' - opposite to the :defer, it compiles the entire package with all
;;             functions and makes them available in the public namespace. It's useful
;;             for packages you need to be ready during the startup Emacs process.
;;  `:bind' - a pleasant way to declare keybinding
;;  `:after' - provides control over the sequence of package loading
;;  `:hook' - a simplified hook declaration, replacing of the traditional
;;     approach with add-hook macros.
;;
;;
;; -------------------------
;; ** About Modes & Hooks **
;; -------------------------
;;
;;  A mode is a code that changes the default behavior. In Emacs we have only
;;  two modes:
;;
;;    `Major' - in fact is a "parser" for a specific language with a specific
;;    semantics. Major mode code describes how we work within the
;;    buffer. For example, the `clojure-mode' package is a Major mode.
;;
;;    `Minor' complements the Major mode and offers additional functionality
;;    beyond the currently edited mode. For example, `yafolding' brings
;;    code folding functionality to Emacs.
;;
;;  `Hook'. The problem with a Minor mode is that it requires manual activation
;;  from the command-menu (M-x) for the specific buffer you’re using. It can’t
;;  be automatically activated for all buffers. Hence, we need to choose where
;;  and when to enable it on a case-by-case basis for the particular buffer or
;;  Major mode.
;;   For example, it comes in handy when you are editing a large file with 400
;;  lines of Clojure code, which can be quite challenging to analyze. Here we
;;  can use 'M-x yafolding-mode' and our current Keymap will be enhanced with
;;  additional bindings (<DEL> is Enter). Once the Major mode is enabled, hooks
;;  are executed. For example, when you open a Clojure file, Emacs automatically
;;  runs the `clojure-mode` and then triggers a `clojure-mode-hook` containing a
;;  list of user-defined hooks.
;;
;;   `C-<DEL>' - yafolding-toggle-element shortens large code inside brackets (...)
;;
;;   `C-S-<DEL>' yafolding-toggle-all performs almost the same as a previous one
;;   but applies to an entire top level function or expression
;;
;;   Anyway, if your editing habits include this experience, you might find
;;  it annoying to enable this mode every time.
;;   Emacs resolves this frustration by adding a `Hook' function available in the
;;  Major mode, which is automatically invoked every time you open the
;;  buffer. Here is an example:
;;
;;      (defun my-clojure-hooks ()
;;        (yafolding-mode +1))
;;
;;      (add-hook 'clojure-mode-hook 'my-clojure-hooks)
;;
;;  Once you've opened the Clojure buffer, enable yafolding-mode. You can
;;  also do it using an inline lambda without defining a dedicated function:
;;
;;       (add-hook 'clojure-mode-hook (lambda ()
;;                                      (yafloding-mode +1)))
;;
;;   Note! Each Minor mode has a different approach for self-enabling or
;;  disabling (by using 't as True, or number greater than 0). To learn how
;;  to do it correctly, use `C-h f' (M-x describe-function) shortcut and 
;;  enter the function name you are searching for.
;;   Supplied arguments MUST be described.
;;   The enabled Minor/Major is indicated on the Modebar.
;;   Read more: https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/elisp/Modes.html
;;
;;
;; ------------------------------------
;; ** Install and configure packages **
;; ------------------------------------
;;
;;   Now, that’s enough text. Let's finally add packages to our EMACS!
;;
;; *** Dashboard
;;    An extendable Emacs buffer that displays useful information during the Emacs
;;   startup. It lists "last used files", opened projects, bookmarks and more.
;;   Read more: https://github.com/emacs-dashboard/emacs-dashboard

(use-package dashboard
  :demand t ;; compile the entire library!
  :ensure t ;; download if not already installed
  :init
  (setq dashboard-week-agenda nil)
  (setq dashboard-set-heading-icons nil)
  (setq dashboard-set-file-icons nil)
  (setq dashboard-page-separator "\n\n")
  (setq dashboard-set-init-info nil)
  (setq dashboard-center-content t)
  (setq dashboard-show-shortcuts t)
  (setq dashboard-set-footer nil)
  (setq dashboard-set-navigator t)
  (setq dashboard-items '((recents  . 15)
			  (projects . 20)
			  (bookmarks . 5)
			  (agenda . 100)))
  :config
  (dashboard-setup-startup-hook))


;; *** Helm
;;    Helm is Emacs incremental completion and selection narrowing
;;   framework (sound like a dissertation topic, right). Comes in 
;;   handy when you finally decide to replace the built-in M-x Emacs
;;   action, which can be hard because of non-intuitive standards.
;;    Helm contains dozens of extensions that add extra functionality to
;;   Emacs.
;;    Read more: https://github.com/emacs-helm/helm

(use-package helm
  :ensure t
  :demand t
  :bind (("M-x" . helm-M-x)
	 ("C-x C-f" . helm-find-files)
	 ("C-." . helm-imenu-in-all-buffers)
	 ("C-x b" . helm-buffers-list)
	 ("C-x r b" . helm-filtered-bookmarks))
  :config
  (helm-mode 1))


;; *** Projectile
;;   Emacs doesn't keep track of opened folders, and it’s too much even for me.
;;   You have two methods to save a Folder as a project directory:
;;
;;    1. Use M-x `projectile-add-known-project' (select the project directory)
;;    2. Add an empty file `.projectile'  to the directory
;;       cd .... & touch `.projectile'
;;
;;   The dashboard extension should display the available projects you’ve added.
;;   Bind Projectile to a shortcut, like 'C-c p'. The following shortcuts
;;   will come in handy:
;;
;;     C-c p f - find file in the project directory
;;     C-c p s s - text searching with SilverSearch
;;     C-c p s g - text searching with gnu/Grep
;;     C-c p r - replace some text in the project
;;
;;   Read more https://github.com/bbatsov/projectile

(use-package projectile
  :ensure t
  :init (projectile-mode +1)
  :bind (:map projectile-mode-map
	      ("C-c p" . projectile-command-map)))


;; *** Helm-projectile
;;   The Helm wrapper, together with Projectile, enhances Projectile's UI and
;;   makes code search more user-friendly by grep, ag,...

(use-package helm-projectile
  :ensure t
  :after (helm projectile)
  :init (helm-projectile-on))

;; *** helm-ag + ag
;;    For searching within a project or system, I recommend using "SilverSearch",
;;   a faster al ternative to grep. It requires the `projectile' package to start.
;;   Read more:
;;    https://github.com/ggreer/the_silver_searcher
;;    https://github.com/emacsorphanage/helm-ag
;;

(use-package ag
